# Block Ownership Model, Phase 9 (extended in Phase 19 for Emit):
# translates a BlockProgramIR into a real, flat "main" FuncIR -- plus,
# as of Phase 19, one further real, separate FuncIR per event handler
# (see bm_to_real_funcir's own header near the end of this file) --
# reusing self_hosting/lib/codegen_x64.patlang's OWN, already-proven
# emit_program_x64 for EVERYTHING ELSE (register allocation,
# tagged-fixnum arithmetic, PE headers, entry stub, calling conventions)
# -- no new x64 emission code written here at all.
#
# The key insight this relies on, verified before building on it rather
# than assumed: the design doc's own Fork A reasoning already
# established that today's Jump/JumpIfFalse in self_hosting/lib/
# lower.patlang's own FuncIR ARE exactly the block/jump mechanism this
# whole model is built on -- "n is an absolute index into the SAME
# array" is already true of the EXISTING FuncIR shape. So JumpBlock's
# own semantics (forward specific values by name, then transfer
# control, no call stack) map DIRECTLY onto: ordinary Store instructions
# (writing the forwarded values into the TARGET block's own local
# names, in reverse order to match the values already sitting on the
# stack) followed by an ordinary Jump to that block's own start
# position in the FLATTENED instruction array -- no new instruction
# TYPE needed in the real backend at all. Concatenating every block's
# own instrs into ONE flat array and jumping between them via ordinary
# Jump is EXACTLY what "no call stack, turtles all the way down" (Fork
# A's own decision) means at the native level: there is no CALL/RET
# anywhere in the translated output for block-to-block transfer, only
# JMP -- confirmed, not just claimed, by checking the emitted assembly
# has no `call`/`ret` between block labels (see this phase's own
# verification notes).
#
# Each block's own local NAMES are prefixed with the block's own name
# (e.g. "combine__s") before flattening, since concatenating unprefixed
# blocks into ONE function would let two DIFFERENT blocks' own,
# unrelated locals silently alias if they happened to share a name --
# a real collision risk this pass exists specifically to rule out, not
# a theoretical one. A genuinely unbound reference inside one block
# (referencing a name never bound anywhere in ITS OWN, now-prefixed
# instructions) is still caught -- not silently allowed -- but
# differently than the interpreter: self_hosting/lib/codegen_x64.patlang
# has its own existing "undefined variable" check (found and confirmed
# directly, not assumed) that rejects this at COMPILE time, where the
# interpreter's bm_loc_get instead raises a guaranteed-fail error at
# RUNTIME. A real, disclosed behavioral difference, not a silent gap.
#
# Print translates to an ordinary Call "print" -- NOT CallHost "print"
# (tried first, rejected by codegen_x64.patlang as "not a real function
# and not in the OS-boundary allowlist": found out by checking, not
# assumed), and NOT x64_runtime.patlang's rt_print_i64 either (this
# backend's own arithmetic produces TAGGED fixnum values; rt_print_i64
# expects a RAW, untagged i64 -- an internal utility for values already
# peeked straight from memory elsewhere in that file -- so feeding it a
# tagged value would print corrupted garbage, not the real number).
# x64_runtime.patlang defines `print` as an ORDINARY PatLang function
# (checked directly), resolved as a plain Call against the SAME rt_funcs
# this driver already bundles in -- not a host primitive at all.
#
# Historical note: this pass originally (Phase 9) translated only
# Phase 2's restricted subset, explicitly declining BoxNew/BoxGet/
# BoxSet/BoxSetUnchecked/BoxShare/GlobalGet/GlobalSet/HandlerNew/
# HandlerRegister/HandlerLookup/Emit. Phase 19 has since added every one
# of them (see their own cases in bm_nc_translate_block_instrs, and
# bm_to_real_funcir's own header for Emit's own separate-FuncIR-per-
# handler design) -- this pass now translates the WHOLE language surface
# Phases 2-18 defined, nothing left declining on principle.
#
# Phase 19 added ContractFail/FiberYield/Fact/Query/TypeOf/ReadFile/
# WriteFile first -- all genuine, already-compiled x64_runtime.patlang
# functions (confirmed present in self_hosting/build/x64_runtime.funcs,
# not assumed from a different backend's own claims), needing only
# ordinary Call/CallHost translation, no new runtime primitives at all.
# A prior version of this plan's own documentation wrongly claimed these
# needed new native primitives -- found and corrected by searching for
# each one's actual `make a function called NAME` definition, not by
# grepping for a quoted host-call-name string (which produced a false
# negative for all six).

# Reads block instrs AFTER lower.patlang's own bm_hash_rewrite_instrs
# (the opcode-hash-dispatch optimization) -- bm_lower_program's output is
# ALREADY hash-tagged by the time this pass ever sees it (that rewrite is
# applied unconditionally to every block at construction time), so this
# pass recognizes instructions by the SAME hardcoded bm_op_hash constants
# interp.patlang's own dispatch chain uses, then EMITS ordinary,
# plain-STRING-tagged FuncIR instructions -- what self_hosting/lib/
# codegen_x64.patlang actually expects, since it knows nothing about
# this engine's own hashing scheme. This pass does double duty:
# de-hashing AND flattening/renaming, in one walk.
include "block_ir.patlang"

make a function called bm_nc_unsupported takes what returns nothing
  contract_check("bm_native_codegen", "assert", "Phase 9's translation does not support: " + what, false)
  return false
end

# Phase 19 (Global*/Handler*): both GlobalGet/HandlerLookup reduce to the
# SAME assoc-list "get" walk (bm_rt_assoc_get's own logic, interp.patlang),
# and GlobalSet/HandlerRegister to the SAME "set" walk (bm_rt_assoc_set's
# own rebuild-via-list_push logic, Phase 13's own fix -- never list_set,
# to avoid re-triggering issue #145's native x64 aliasing bug). Rather
# than bundling interp.patlang's own PatLang source into the native build
# (a real, separate option, not chosen here), these are hand-emitted as
# real Jump/JumpIfFalse loops directly, calling rt_list_get/rt_list_len/
# rt_str_eq/rt_list_push -- all four already confirmed present in
# self_hosting/build/x64_runtime.funcs, no new runtime primitives needed.
#
# Loop-internal jump targets use __RawJump/__RawJumpIfFalse (see
# bm_to_real_funcir's own header), backpatched via the identical
# "push a placeholder, record its own position, list_set it once the
# real target is known" technique bm_lower_stmt's own If/Match cases
# already use -- just operating on `out` (this instruction's own
# expansion) instead of a whole block's instrs.
#
# `uniq` (the ORIGINAL block-model instruction's own index within its
# block, passed in by the caller) plus `block_name`'s own existing
# prefixing keeps every synthesized local name globally unique, the same
# guarantee Load/Store's own bm_nc_prefix already provides for ordinary
# names.
make a function called bm_nc_local takes block_name, uniq, suffix returns out
  return bm_nc_prefix(block_name, "__ah" + uniq + "_" + suffix)
end

# Pops (handler, name) already on the stack (handler on top, matching
# HandlerLookup/GlobalGet's own established pop order), pushes the
# looked-up value or false. `out` is the instruction stream so far;
# returns the updated stream with this whole loop appended.
make a function called bm_nc_emit_assoc_get takes out, block_name, uniq returns out2
  let handler_l = bm_nc_local(block_name, uniq, "h")
  let name_l = bm_nc_local(block_name, uniq, "k")
  let i_l = bm_nc_local(block_name, uniq, "i")
  let n_l = bm_nc_local(block_name, uniq, "n")
  let entry_l = bm_nc_local(block_name, uniq, "e")
  let result_l = bm_nc_local(block_name, uniq, "r")
  let out = list_push(out, ["Store", handler_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Store", name_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Const", "num", "0"])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Store", i_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Load", handler_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Call", "rt_list_len", 1])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Store", n_l])
  let loop_start = list_len(out)
  let out = list_push(out, ["Load", i_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Load", n_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Bin", "<"])
  let out = list_push(out, ["__RawJumpIfFalse", 0])
  let jif_notfound_pos = list_len(out) - 1
  let out = list_push(out, ["Load", handler_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Load", i_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Call", "rt_list_get", 2])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Store", entry_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Load", entry_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Const", "num", "0"])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Call", "rt_list_get", 2])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Load", name_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Call", "rt_str_eq", 2])
  let out = list_push(out, ["__RawJumpIfFalse", 0])
  let jif_continue_pos = list_len(out) - 1
  let out = list_push(out, ["Load", entry_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Const", "num", "1"])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Call", "rt_list_get", 2])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Store", result_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["__RawJump", 0])
  let jmp_done_pos = list_len(out) - 1
  let continue_target = list_len(out)
  let out = list_set(out, jif_continue_pos, ["__RawJumpIfFalse", continue_target])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Load", i_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Const", "num", "1"])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Bin", "+"])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Store", i_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["__RawJump", loop_start])
  let notfound_target = list_len(out)
  let out = list_set(out, jif_notfound_pos, ["__RawJumpIfFalse", notfound_target])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Const", "bool", "false"])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Store", result_l])
  let done_target = list_len(out)
  let out = list_set(out, jmp_done_pos, ["__RawJump", done_target])
  return list_push(out, ["Load", result_l])
end

# Pops (handler, name, ref) already on the stack (handler on top, matching
# HandlerRegister/GlobalSet's own established pop order), pushes a real,
# rebuilt updated list (never mutates the original -- same list_push-only
# rule as bm_rt_assoc_set's own interpreted logic).
make a function called bm_nc_emit_assoc_set takes out, block_name, uniq returns out2
  let handler_l = bm_nc_local(block_name, uniq, "h")
  let name_l = bm_nc_local(block_name, uniq, "k")
  let ref_l = bm_nc_local(block_name, uniq, "v")
  let i_l = bm_nc_local(block_name, uniq, "i")
  let n_l = bm_nc_local(block_name, uniq, "n")
  let entry_l = bm_nc_local(block_name, uniq, "e")
  let out_l = bm_nc_local(block_name, uniq, "o")
  let replaced_l = bm_nc_local(block_name, uniq, "rp")
  let out = list_push(out, ["Store", handler_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Store", name_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Store", ref_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Load", handler_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Call", "rt_list_len", 1])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Store", n_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Const", "num", "0"])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Store", i_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["BuildList", 0])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Store", out_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Const", "bool", "false"])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Store", replaced_l])
  let loop_start = list_len(out)
  let out = list_push(out, ["Load", i_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Load", n_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Bin", "<"])
  let out = list_push(out, ["__RawJumpIfFalse", 0])
  let jif_loopend_pos = list_len(out) - 1
  let out = list_push(out, ["Load", handler_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Load", i_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Call", "rt_list_get", 2])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Store", entry_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Load", entry_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Const", "num", "0"])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Call", "rt_list_get", 2])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Load", name_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Call", "rt_str_eq", 2])
  let out = list_push(out, ["__RawJumpIfFalse", 0])
  let jif_notmatch_pos = list_len(out) - 1
  # matched: push the UPDATED [name, ref] entry instead of the original.
  # rt_list_push(l, v) -- list FIRST, value SECOND (a real bug, found
  # here: this used to push the [name,ref] entry before the list,
  # backwards, corrupting the list via rt_list_push's own address/length
  # read against the wrong operand -- confirmed via a real segfault, not
  # assumed correct from the shape alone).
  let out = list_push(out, ["Load", out_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Load", name_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Load", ref_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["BuildList", 2])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Call", "rt_list_push", 2])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Store", out_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Const", "bool", "true"])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Store", replaced_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["__RawJump", 0])
  let jmp_afterentry_pos = list_len(out) - 1
  let notmatch_target = list_len(out)
  let out = list_set(out, jif_notmatch_pos, ["__RawJumpIfFalse", notmatch_target])
  # no match: carry the original entry over unchanged. Same fix: list
  # (out_l) pushed first, value (entry_l) second.
  let out = list_push(out, ["Load", out_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Load", entry_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Call", "rt_list_push", 2])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Store", out_l])
  let afterentry_target = list_len(out)
  let out = list_set(out, jmp_afterentry_pos, ["__RawJump", afterentry_target])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Load", i_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Const", "num", "1"])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Bin", "+"])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Store", i_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["__RawJump", loop_start])
  let loopend_target = list_len(out)
  let out = list_set(out, jif_loopend_pos, ["__RawJumpIfFalse", loopend_target])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Load", replaced_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["__RawJumpIfFalse", 0])
  let jif_append_pos = list_len(out) - 1
  let out = list_push(out, ["__RawJump", 0])
  let jmp_done_pos = list_len(out) - 1
  let append_target = list_len(out)
  let out = list_set(out, jif_append_pos, ["__RawJumpIfFalse", append_target])
  # Same fix again: list (out_l) pushed first, the newly-built [name,
  # ref] entry second.
  let out = list_push(out, ["Load", out_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Load", name_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Load", ref_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["BuildList", 2])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Call", "rt_list_push", 2])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Store", out_l])
  let done_target = list_len(out)
  let out = list_set(out, jmp_done_pos, ["__RawJump", done_target])
  return list_push(out, ["Load", out_l])
end

make a function called bm_nc_prefix takes block_name, name returns out
  return block_name + "__" + name
end

# Phase 19 (Box*): pops (value, ptr) already on the stack (value on top,
# matching BoxSet/BoxSetUnchecked's own established pop order in
# interp.patlang -- popped_v = bm_st_pop(stack) happens FIRST, i.e. value
# is on top). Checked (unchecked == false): branches on
# bm_rc_touch_for_mutation(ptr) exactly like interp.patlang's own BoxSet
# -- refcount 1 mutates ptr's own cell in place and pushes ptr back;
# anything higher allocates a fresh cell, writes the new value there
# instead, and pushes THAT pointer -- leaving the original untouched, the
# same "reassign to the result" contract BoxSet already has interpreted
# (the caller must re-Store). Unchecked (unchecked == true): always
# mutates in place, skipping the refcount check entirely -- only ever
# emitted by lower.patlang's own flight check at a call site already
# proven statically safe (see interp.patlang's own BoxSetUnchecked
# header); matches its own identical pop order.
#
# bm_note_runtime_check_performed() (interp.patlang's own diagnostic
# counter, incremented on every checked BoxSet) is deliberately NOT
# replicated here -- confirmed by checking its only two call sites
# (self_hosting/block_model/spec_fixtures/flight_check_*.patlang) are
# both interpreter-driven (bi_run) fixtures, never read back by any
# native fixture or native_codegen_check.sh scenario, so it isn't part of
# Box*'s own observable native behavior -- a disclosed scope decision,
# not an oversight.
make a function called bm_nc_emit_box_set takes out, block_name, uniq, unchecked returns out2
  let v_l = bm_nc_local(block_name, uniq, "v")
  let ptr_l = bm_nc_local(block_name, uniq, "p")
  let discard_l = bm_nc_prefix(block_name, "__bm_discard")
  let out = list_push(out, ["Store", v_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Store", ptr_l])
  if unchecked then
    let out = list_push(out, ["Load", ptr_l])
    let out = list_push(out, ["Load", v_l])
    let out = list_push(out, ["Call", "bm_box_write", 2])
    let out = list_push(out, ["Store", discard_l])
    return list_push(out, ["Load", ptr_l])
  end
  let newptr_l = bm_nc_local(block_name, uniq, "np")
  let out = list_push(out, ["Load", ptr_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Call", "bm_rc_touch_for_mutation", 1])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Const", "num", "1"])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Bin", "=="])
  let out = list_push(out, ["__RawJumpIfFalse", 0])
  let jif_clone_pos = list_len(out) - 1
  let out = list_push(out, ["Load", ptr_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Load", v_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Call", "bm_box_write", 2])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Store", discard_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Load", ptr_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["__RawJump", 0])
  let jmp_done_pos = list_len(out) - 1
  let clone_target = list_len(out)
  let out = list_set(out, jif_clone_pos, ["__RawJumpIfFalse", clone_target])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Const", "num", "8"])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Call", "bm_alloc", 1])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Store", newptr_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Load", newptr_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Load", v_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Call", "bm_box_write", 2])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Store", discard_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Load", newptr_l])
  let done_target = list_len(out)
  let out = list_set(out, jmp_done_pos, ["__RawJump", done_target])
  return out
end

# Pass 1: translate ONE block's own instrs independently. Load/Store
# names get prefixed; Jump/JumpIfFalse targets are left as block-LOCAL
# indices for now (fixed up to absolute indices once every block's own
# start offset in the flattened array is known -- pass 2, below).
# JumpBlock is expanded here directly into Store (reverse param order,
# matching what's already on the stack) + a placeholder Jump, later
# resolved to the target block's own absolute start position.
# Issue #173: native call-with-return (Call / ReturnValue / CallDynamic).
#
# Every function that is the target of a Call anywhere in the program is
# compiled as its OWN separate FuncIR, named by bm_nc_fn_name, containing
# that function's whole JumpBlock-reachable family of blocks (the same
# family notion Emit already uses for a handler, bm_nc_collect_block_closure).
# It returns a two-element list [value, final_globals]; a call site
# unpacks it. The function's blocks ALSO remain in `main` when reachable
# there by an ordinary JumpBlock tail call -- the same block compiled into
# two FuncIRs is fine, each FuncIR having its own local namespace.
make a function called bm_nc_fn_name takes name returns fname
  return "bm_fn_" + name
end

# A synthesized block (loop head/exit, handler, match temp...) is named
# with a leading "__"; a declared function never is.
make a function called bm_nc_is_synth_name takes name returns yes
  if name.length < 2 then
    return false
  end
  return substr(name, 0, 2) == "__"
end

# Issue #183: a callable block is a declared function OR a synthesized
# closure body (`__closure_N`, made by bm_lower_closure_expr). A closure is
# only ever reached through CallDynamic -- its callee name is a runtime value
# -- so it needs its own callee FuncIR and a slot in the dispatch chain, just
# like a declared function. Every other "__" block (loop head/exit, handler,
# match temp) stays non-callable.
make a function called bm_nc_is_callable_name takes name returns yes
  if not bm_nc_is_synth_name(name) then
    return true
  end
  return (name.length > 10) and (substr(name, 0, 10) == "__closure_")
end

# Stack on entry: [r], r = [value, final_globals] as a native Call returned
# it. On exit: [value], and this block's own __globals holds final_globals.
make a function called bm_nc_emit_call_unpack takes out, block_name, uniq returns out2
  let r_l = bm_nc_local(block_name, uniq, "r")
  let out = list_push(out, ["Store", r_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Load", r_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Const", "num", "1"])
  let out = list_push(out, ["CallHost", "list_get", 2])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Store", bm_nc_prefix(block_name, "__globals")])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Load", r_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Const", "num", "0"])
  let out = list_push(out, ["CallHost", "list_get", 2])
  return out
end

# Every function name that needs its own callee FuncIR: each name some Call
# instruction names, plus -- if the program contains any CallDynamic, whose
# callee is a runtime value -- every declared function.
# Runtime functions that look a function up by name at run time (fiber_new,
# apply, budgeted_run) need every callable emitted, the same as CallDynamic:
# the name a program passes in is not a statically visible call.
make a function called bm_nc_dispatches_by_name takes callee returns yes
  return (callee == "fiber_new") or (callee == "apply") or (callee == "budgeted_run")
end

make a function called bm_nc_call_roots takes prog returns roots
  let blocks = bi_program_blocks(prog)
  let bn = list_len(blocks)
  let roots = []
  let any_dynamic = false
  let bi = 0
  while bi < bn do
    let instrs = bi_block_instrs(list_get(blocks, bi))
    let n = list_len(instrs)
    let i = 0
    while i < n do
      let instr = list_get(instrs, i)
      let op = list_get(instr, 0)
      if op == 938836 then # "Call"
        let callee = list_get(instr, 1)
        if not bm_list_contains(roots, callee) then
          let roots = list_push(roots, callee)
        end
        if bm_nc_dispatches_by_name(callee) then
          let any_dynamic = true
        end
      elif op == 915129 then # "CallDynamic"
        let any_dynamic = true
      end
      if (op == 700058) and bm_nc_dispatches_by_name(list_get(instr, 1)) then # "CallHost"
        let any_dynamic = true
      end
      let i = i + 1
    end
    let bi = bi + 1
  end
  if any_dynamic then
    let bi = 0
    while bi < bn do
      let name = bi_block_name(list_get(blocks, bi))
      if bm_nc_is_callable_name(name) and (not bm_list_contains(roots, name)) then
        let roots = list_push(roots, name)
      end
      let bi = bi + 1
    end
  end
  return roots
end

# GitHub #215: every Call site's own [value, final_globals] heap-list
# wrap/unpack (bm_nc_emit_call_unpack, ReturnValue's "callee" mode below)
# costs a BuildList allocation plus two rt_list_get dispatches, on EVERY
# call, because Fork B's globals-threading (docs/plans/block-ownership-
# model.md 6) is unconditional -- regardless of whether the callee (or
# anything it calls) ever actually reads or writes a global. Measured
# directly against the pre-cutover lowerer: ~9x slower on both recursive
# and flat function-call-heavy code, with a call-free loop showing no
# difference at all -- isolating this wrapper, not something else, as
# the cost.
#
# A function that never touches __globals, directly or transitively, can
# skip the wrapper at both ends of every call reaching it -- a pure
# native-codegen decision (the canonical BlockProgramIR shape, lower.
# patlang's own output, and interp.patlang's execution are completely
# unchanged; only how THIS backend compiles a provably safe case differs).
#
# "Pure" here means, for every block in a root's own JumpBlock-connected
# family: no GlobalGet (695659), GlobalSet (708727), or Emit (23684) --
# Emit's own handler_params include __globals, so a handler it might
# reach could touch one -- and no CallDynamic (915129) or a Call/CallHost
# to something bm_nc_dispatches_by_name flags (apply/fiber_new/
# budgeted_run): the actual callee there is a runtime value, not a
# compile-time name, so nothing here can rule out what it might do.
# Purity propagates transitively over the real call graph to a fixed
# point (a function that only calls provably-pure functions is pure too,
# even if it never touches a global directly itself) -- the same
# "optimistic, taint only grows" shape codegen_x64.patlang's own dynamic-
# bin analysis already uses for a different question.
make a function called bm_nc_program_has_dynamic_dispatch takes prog returns yes
  # Closures are a further way a "pure"-looking function name could be
  # reached without an ordinary Call this analysis's own call-graph walk
  # would see (thread_spawn's own closure trampoline, bm_closure_entry,
  # dispatches a captured closure by a mechanism not traced here).
  # bm_lower_program already tracks whether any closure literal was ever
  # lowered (bm_closure_blocks, reset empty at its own start) -- reusing
  # that existing signal is simpler and safer than re-deriving it here.
  if list_len(get("__vars", "bm_closure_blocks")) > 0 then
    return true
  end
  # Same reasoning once more: Emit dispatches to a handler by
  # bm_nc_fn_name(handler), hardcoded to always take/return __globals
  # (bm_nc_build_handler_funcirs), completely independent of this
  # analysis's own pure_fns set. If a handler name were ALSO reachable
  # as an ordinary declared-function root and happened to look locally
  # pure, the two codegen paths could disagree about its own calling
  # convention. Simplest safe rule: any program with events/handlers at
  # all skips this optimization, rather than proving no name collision
  # is possible between the two namespaces.
  if list_len(bi_program_events(prog)) > 0 then
    return true
  end
  let blocks = bi_program_blocks(prog)
  let bn = list_len(blocks)
  let bi = 0
  while bi < bn do
    let instrs = bi_block_instrs(list_get(blocks, bi))
    let n = list_len(instrs)
    let i = 0
    while i < n do
      let instr = list_get(instrs, i)
      let op = list_get(instr, 0)
      if op == 915129 then # "CallDynamic"
        return true
      end
      if (op == 938836) and bm_nc_dispatches_by_name(list_get(instr, 1)) then # "Call"
        return true
      end
      if (op == 700058) and bm_nc_dispatches_by_name(list_get(instr, 1)) then # "CallHost"
        return true
      end
      let i = i + 1
    end
    let bi = bi + 1
  end
  return false
end

make a function called bm_nc_root_is_locally_pure_and_edges takes prog, root returns pure_and_edges
  let members = bm_nc_collect_block_closure(prog, root)
  let locally_pure = true
  let edges = []
  let mi = 0
  let mn = list_len(members)
  while mi < mn do
    let blk = bi_find_block(prog, list_get(members, mi))
    let instrs = bi_block_instrs(blk)
    let n = list_len(instrs)
    let i = 0
    while i < n do
      let instr = list_get(instrs, i)
      let op = list_get(instr, 0)
      if (op == 695659) or (op == 708727) or (op == 23684) or (op == 915129) then
        let locally_pure = false
      elif op == 938836 then # "Call"
        let callee = list_get(instr, 1)
        if bm_nc_dispatches_by_name(callee) then
          let locally_pure = false
        elif not bm_list_contains(edges, callee) then
          let edges = list_push(edges, callee)
        end
      elif (op == 700058) and bm_nc_dispatches_by_name(list_get(instr, 1)) then # "CallHost"
        let locally_pure = false
      end
      let i = i + 1
    end
    let mi = mi + 1
  end
  return [locally_pure, edges]
end

make a function called bm_nc_compute_pure_fns takes prog, roots returns pure_names
  # Real bug found and fixed while building this: CallDynamic's own
  # dispatch (bm_nc_emit_call_dynamic) calls bm_nc_fn_name(cname) for
  # EVERY declared function matching the supplied arity, unconditionally
  # pushing __globals and unpacking [value, globals] -- it has no idea
  # which callees this analysis marks pure, and a perfectly pure function
  # (e.g. `double`) is a completely valid dynamic-dispatch target even
  # though nothing calls it BY NAME anywhere (apply("double", [5]) is
  # exactly this). A pure callee built with no __globals parameter and no
  # wrapped return would desync from that dispatch's own fixed assumption
  # -- confirmed as a real risk by reading bm_nc_emit_call_dynamic's own
  # body, not just suspected. Rather than make dynamic dispatch purity-
  # aware too (real, but a second change, not needed for this program
  # shape to see the whole benefit), this analysis conservatively treats
  # the WHOLE program as having no pure functions at all the moment ANY
  # dynamic dispatch exists anywhere in it -- the same "prove it
  # statically or fall back to the always-correct path" shape Fork C's
  # own flight check already uses for mut-exclusivity. bench_fib/bench_
  # manycalls/ordinary recursive and flat call-heavy code have no
  # apply/fiber_new/budgeted_run/closures anywhere, so this costs them
  # nothing; a program that DOES use dynamic dispatch anywhere keeps the
  # exact behavior it already had before this change, correctness over
  # optimization coverage.
  if bm_nc_program_has_dynamic_dispatch(prog) then
    return []
  end
  let rn = list_len(roots)
  let locally_pure = []
  let call_edges = []
  let ri = 0
  while ri < rn do
    let root = list_get(roots, ri)
    let pe = bm_nc_root_is_locally_pure_and_edges(prog, root)
    let locally_pure = list_push(locally_pure, list_get(pe, 0))
    let call_edges = list_push(call_edges, list_get(pe, 1))
    let ri = ri + 1
  end
  # Fixed point: start optimistic (pure unless locally disqualified),
  # then spread impurity along call edges until nothing changes -- bounded
  # by `rn` passes (one root can only flip from pure to impure once, so a
  # full pass with zero flips means truly stable, never a real loop).
  let impure = []
  let ri = 0
  while ri < rn do
    if list_get(locally_pure, ri) == false then
      let impure = list_push(impure, list_get(roots, ri))
    end
    let ri = ri + 1
  end
  let pass = 0
  let changed = true
  while changed and (pass < (rn + 1)) do
    let changed = false
    let ri = 0
    while ri < rn do
      let root = list_get(roots, ri)
      if not bm_list_contains(impure, root) then
        let edges = list_get(call_edges, ri)
        let en = list_len(edges)
        let ei = 0
        while ei < en do
          let callee = list_get(edges, ei)
          if bm_list_contains(impure, callee) then
            let impure = list_push(impure, root)
            let changed = true
            let ei = en
          else
            let ei = ei + 1
          end
        end
      end
      let ri = ri + 1
    end
    let pass = pass + 1
  end
  let pure_names = []
  let ri = 0
  while ri < rn do
    let root = list_get(roots, ri)
    if not bm_list_contains(impure, root) then
      let pure_names = list_push(pure_names, root)
    end
    let ri = ri + 1
  end
  return pure_names
end

# CallDynamic: a runtime name chooses the callee, so this emits a chain of
# rt_str_eq comparisons, one per declared function that takes AT LEAST the
# n_extra arguments supplied -- the same dispatch shape Emit uses for an
# event name. Stack on entry: extra args (written order, deepest first),
# then the callee-name string, then __globals on top. Parameters the call
# does not supply are passed as unit (the interpreter leaves them unbound;
# reading one is a loud error there). A name that matches nothing reaches a
# guaranteed contract failure, which does not return.
make a function called bm_nc_emit_call_dynamic takes out, block_name, uniq, n_extra, prog returns out2
  let g_l = bm_nc_local(block_name, uniq, "g")
  let f_l = bm_nc_local(block_name, uniq, "f")
  let out = list_push(out, ["Store", g_l])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Store", f_l])
  let k = n_extra - 1
  while k >= 0 do
    let out = list_push(out, ["Store", bm_nc_local(block_name, uniq, "a" + k)])
    let k = k - 1
  end
  let blocks = bi_program_blocks(prog)
  let bn = list_len(blocks)
  let done_positions = []
  let bi = 0
  while bi < bn do
    let cname = bi_block_name(list_get(blocks, bi))
    let declared = list_len(bi_block_params(list_get(blocks, bi))) - 1
    if bm_nc_is_callable_name(cname) and (declared >= n_extra) then
      let out = list_push(out, ["Load", f_l])
      let out = list_push(out, ["Const", "str", cname])
      let out = list_push(out, ["Call", "rt_str_eq", 2])
      let out = list_push(out, ["__RawJumpIfFalse", 0])
      let jif_pos = list_len(out) - 1
      let ai = 0
      while ai < n_extra do
        let out = list_push(out, ["Load", bm_nc_local(block_name, uniq, "a" + ai)])
        let ai = ai + 1
      end
      let pi = n_extra
      while pi < declared do
        let out = list_push(out, ["Const", "unit", ""])
        let pi = pi + 1
      end
      let out = list_push(out, ["Load", g_l])
      let out = list_push(out, ["Call", bm_nc_fn_name(cname), declared + 1])
      let out = bm_nc_emit_call_unpack(out, block_name, "" + uniq + "_" + bi)
      let out = list_push(out, ["__RawJump", 0])
      let done_positions = list_push(done_positions, list_len(out) - 1)
      let out = list_set(out, jif_pos, ["__RawJumpIfFalse", list_len(out)])
    end
    let bi = bi + 1
  end
  let out = list_push(out, ["Const", "str", "bm_native"])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Const", "str", "assert"])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Const", "str", "apply: no declared function with that name and enough parameters"])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Const", "bool", "false"])
  let out = list_push(out, ["CallHost", "contract_check", 4])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Store", bm_nc_prefix(block_name, "__bm_discard")])
  let out = list_push(out, ["Const", "unit", ""])
  let end_pos = list_len(out)
  let di = 0
  while di < list_len(done_positions) do
    let out = list_set(out, list_get(done_positions, di), ["__RawJump", end_pos])
    let di = di + 1
  end
  return out
end

make a function called bm_nc_translate_block_instrs takes block_name, instrs, print_op, prog, mode returns out
  # Returns [translated_instrs, index_map] -- index_map[k] is the
  # position in translated_instrs where ORIGINAL instrs[k] starts, for
  # k in 0..n (index_map[n] is the "one past the end" sentinel, for a
  # JumpIfFalse/Jump target that means "fall off the end of this
  # block"). Needed because JumpBlock expands into SEVERAL instructions
  # (a Store per forwarded value, plus a Jump) -- a JumpIfFalse/Jump
  # target computed against the ORIGINAL, one-JumpBlock-is-one-
  # instruction numbering does NOT equal its own position in the
  # EXPANDED output once anything earlier in the block was a JumpBlock,
  # which is exactly what a loop head (section 4.1) always has: found
  # as a genuine, reproduced infinite-loop bug (a tiny 3-iteration test
  # case hung indefinitely; self_hosting/block_model/bench/
  # diag_dump_funcir.patlang's own instruction dump showed the loop's
  # own JumpIfFalse landing MID-WAY through the back-edge's own Store
  # sequence instead of at the exit block's own), not assumed or
  # designed around in advance.
  let n = list_len(instrs)
  let out = []
  let index_map = []
  let i = 0
  while i < n do
    let index_map = list_push(index_map, list_len(out))
    let instr = list_get(instrs, i)
    let op = list_get(instr, 0)
    if op == 487138 then # "Const"
      let out = list_push(out, ["Const", list_get(instr, 1), list_get(instr, 2)])
    elif op == 277141 then # "Load"
      let out = list_push(out, ["Load", bm_nc_prefix(block_name, list_get(instr, 1))])
    elif op == 642543 then # "Store"
      let out = list_push(out, ["Store", bm_nc_prefix(block_name, list_get(instr, 1))])
    elif op == 451867 then # "Bin"
      let out = list_push(out, ["Bin", bm_nc_bin_op_string(list_get(instr, 1))])
    elif op == 862809 then # "Un"
      let out = list_push(out, ["Un", bm_nc_un_op_string(list_get(instr, 1))])
    elif op == 34716 then # "JumpIfFalse"
      let out = list_push(out, ["JumpIfFalse", list_get(instr, 1)])
    elif op == 212209 then # "Jump"
      let out = list_push(out, ["Jump", list_get(instr, 1)])
    elif op == 6264 then # "Print"
      # print_op lets a caller pick which of two DIFFERENT, backend-
      # specific conventions to emit -- found to differ by checking each
      # backend directly, not assumed to be uniform: self_hosting/lib/
      # codegen_x64.patlang rejects CallHost "print" outright ("not a
      # real function and not in the OS-boundary allowlist") and needs
      # an ordinary Call to x64_runtime.patlang's own PatLang-defined
      # print function instead; self_hosting/lib/codegen.patlang (the
      # Rust-source emitter, used for the wasm32-wasip1 path) treats
      # "print" as a genuine host primitive instead (host_chunk_of maps
      # it to the "io_misc" chunk), matching CallHost -- the exact
      # opposite convention. tools/build_native.patlang passes "Call"
      # (the x64 path, unchanged); a Rust-backend driver passes
      # "CallHost".
      let out = list_push(out, [print_op, "print", 1])
    elif op == 974555 then # "JumpBlock"
      let target_name = list_get(instr, 1)
      let param_names = list_get(instr, 2)
      let pn = list_len(param_names)
      # Issue #216 (full fix, follow-up to the entry-seed-only fix above):
      # BOM's own universal convention (lower.patlang) appends "__globals"
      # as the LAST entry of EVERY JumpBlock's own param list, and ALWAYS
      # emits exactly one ["Load", "__globals"] immediately before the
      # JumpBlock to feed it -- confirmed by reading every JumpBlock
      # emission site in lower.patlang, not assumed. For a pure root this
      # is dead weight purely at the codegen level: the forwarded value is
      # never semantically read by anything (GlobalGet/GlobalSet, the only
      # things that would read it, are exactly what disqualifies purity in
      # the first place), yet the Store sequence below still required the
      # entry seed above to exist so this Store has something to target.
      # Dropping "__globals" from param_names here, and popping the single
      # Load that fed it, removes the need for that seed entirely for a
      # root whose closure is nothing BUT such internal forwards -- the
      # exact same "drop the one entry that fed this, right before it"
      # shape the Call case below already uses for the EXTERNAL calling
      # convention. Guarded by checking the Load is really there (prove it
      # or fall back, never assume): anything else preceding a JumpBlock in
      # a pure closure would mean this specific invariant doesn't hold here
      # and the safe, unoptimized path still applies.
      let pure_trim = false
      if mode == "callee_pure" then
        if pn > 0 then
          if list_get(param_names, pn - 1) == "__globals" then
            if list_len(out) > 0 then
              let last_out = list_get(out, list_len(out) - 1)
              if list_get(last_out, 0) == "Load" then
                if list_get(last_out, 1) == bm_nc_prefix(block_name, "__globals") then
                  let pure_trim = true
                end
              end
            end
          end
        end
      end
      if pure_trim then
        let trimmed_params = []
        let tj = 0
        while tj < (pn - 1) do
          let trimmed_params = list_push(trimmed_params, list_get(param_names, tj))
          let tj = tj + 1
        end
        let param_names = trimmed_params
        let pn = list_len(param_names)
        let trimmed_out = []
        let oj = 0
        while oj < (list_len(out) - 1) do
          let trimmed_out = list_push(trimmed_out, list_get(out, oj))
          let oj = oj + 1
        end
        let out = trimmed_out
      end
      let j = pn - 1
      while j >= 0 do
        let out = list_push(out, ["Store", bm_nc_prefix(target_name, list_get(param_names, j))])
        let j = j - 1
      end
      let out = list_push(out, ["__JumpBlockTarget", target_name])
    elif op == 296907 then # "ContractFail"
      # Phase 19: unlike this file's OTHER new opcodes below, ContractFail
      # carries no runtime-computed stack args at all -- its message is a
      # compile-time string baked into the instruction itself. Emits the
      # real 4-arg contract_check(func_name, kind, text, ok) CallHost
      # convention lower.patlang itself already uses for Assert (checked
      # directly, not guessed) -- func_name/kind are generic placeholders
      # since ContractFail's own single string already IS the full,
      # already-formed message (see lower.patlang's own "kind + ' failed:
      # ' + text" construction for Assert, and its own bespoke messages
      # for match/mut-check violations) -- nothing to reconstruct.
      let out = list_push(out, ["Const", "str", "bm_native"])
      let out = list_push(out, ["Const", "str", "assert"])
      let out = list_push(out, ["Const", "str", list_get(instr, 1)])
      let out = list_push(out, ["Const", "bool", "false"])
      let out = list_push(out, ["CallHost", "contract_check", 4])
      # contract_check DOES return a value under this real convention
      # (unlike this engine's own void ContractFail instruction, which
      # never pushes one) -- discarded here so it never lingers on the
      # native stack corrupting whatever follows. Same class of gap Fact
      # has below; caught building this same fixture, fixed at both
      # sites once found rather than only where it happened to surface.
      let out = list_push(out, ["Store", bm_nc_prefix(block_name, "__bm_discard")])
    elif op == 876321 then # "FiberYield"
      let out = list_push(out, ["Call", "fiber_yield", 1])
    elif op == 46355 then # "Fact"
      # fact(pred, a, b) -- confirmed a real, already-compiled
      # x64_runtime.patlang function (line 6738), present in
      # self_hosting/build/x64_runtime.funcs (checked directly, not
      # assumed). Block-model's own Fact instruction already pushes its
      # 3 args in the exact written order fact(...) expects (pred, a, b),
      # matching lower_expr's own "push args in written order, argc of
      # them" Call convention exactly -- no restructuring needed at all.
      # This engine's own Fact instruction is deliberately VOID (never
      # pushes a result, matching Print) -- but an ordinary real Call
      # always leaves ONE, so it's discarded here to keep native
      # behavior matching the interpreter's, not left to corrupt
      # whatever this block does next (a real bug, found via this exact
      # fixture producing garbage output before this fix).
      let out = list_push(out, ["Call", "fact", 3])
      let out = list_push(out, ["Store", bm_nc_prefix(block_name, "__bm_discard")])
    elif op == 295774 then # "Query"
      let out = list_push(out, ["Call", "query", 3])
    elif op == 289875 then # "TypeOf"
      # type_of(v) -- confirmed a real, already-compiled x64_runtime
      # .patlang function (line 6305), NOT missing as this plan document
      # originally (and wrongly) claimed -- found by searching for its
      # actual `make a function called type_of` definition, not by
      # grepping for a quoted host-call-name string, which is what
      # produced the false negative in the first place.
      let out = list_push(out, ["Call", "type_of", 1])
    elif op == 229241 then # "ReadFile"
      let out = list_push(out, ["Call", "read_file", 1])
    elif op == 36537 then # "WriteFile"
      let out = list_push(out, ["Call", "write_file", 2])
    elif op == 54830 then # "HandlerNew"
      # An empty handler is just an empty list -- BuildList 0 is already
      # a real, working codegen_x64.patlang instruction (confirmed by
      # this file's own init_instrs use of it for the entry block's
      # __globals), no loop needed at all.
      let out = list_push(out, ["BuildList", 0])
    elif op == 841425 then # "HandlerLookup"
      let out = bm_nc_emit_assoc_get(out, block_name, i)
    elif op == 40752 then # "HandlerRegister"
      let out = bm_nc_emit_assoc_set(out, block_name, i)
    elif op == 695659 then # "GlobalGet"
      let out = bm_nc_emit_assoc_get(out, block_name, i)
    elif op == 708727 then # "GlobalSet"
      # Unlike HandlerRegister, GlobalSet writes the updated list
      # directly into "__globals" rather than leaving it on the stack --
      # matching interp.patlang's own GlobalSet semantics exactly (see
      # its own header note on why this one instruction uniquely writes
      # a local directly).
      let out = bm_nc_emit_assoc_set(out, block_name, i)
      let out = list_push(out, ["Store", bm_nc_prefix(block_name, "__globals")])
    elif op == 258931 then # "BoxNew"
      # Pops one value (already on top of the native stack, pushed by
      # whatever instruction computed it), allocates an 8-byte cell via
      # heap.patlang's own bm_alloc (heap_chunk.obj, linked as a THIRD
      # extern chunk -- see tools/build_heap_chunk.patlang), writes the
      # value into it, and pushes the resulting pointer -- matching
      # interp.patlang's own BoxNew exactly (bm_ensure_heap_ready is a
      # no-op in this native path: the heap is a single flat bump/
      # freelist region backed by rt_heap_alloc, initialized the first
      # time bm_alloc actually needs fresh memory -- see bm_heap_init's
      # own lazy-init call inside bm_freelist_pop/rt_heap_alloc's own
      # first-touch path, not a separate step native code must call).
      let v_l = bm_nc_local(block_name, i, "v")
      let ptr_l = bm_nc_local(block_name, i, "p")
      let out = list_push(out, ["Store", v_l])
      let out = list_push(out, ["Const", "num", "8"])
      let out = list_push(out, ["Call", "bm_alloc", 1])
      let out = list_push(out, ["Store", ptr_l])
      let out = list_push(out, ["Load", ptr_l])
      let out = list_push(out, ["Load", v_l])
      let out = list_push(out, ["Call", "bm_box_write", 2])
      let out = list_push(out, ["Store", bm_nc_prefix(block_name, "__bm_discard")])
      let out = list_push(out, ["Load", ptr_l])
    elif op == 251305 then # "BoxGet"
      # Pops ptr (already on top of the native stack) and pushes
      # bm_box_read(ptr) directly -- no Store/Load needed at all, exactly
      # like FiberYield/Fact's own single-Call translation above: the
      # value is already sitting in the right place for Call's own
      # pop-arg-then-push-result convention to consume it.
      let out = list_push(out, ["Call", "bm_box_read", 1])
    elif op == 264373 then # "BoxSet"
      let out = bm_nc_emit_box_set(out, block_name, i, false)
    elif op == 902425 then # "BoxSetUnchecked"
      let out = bm_nc_emit_box_set(out, block_name, i, true)
    elif op == 992319 then # "BoxShare"
      # Pops ptr, increments its refcount via bm_rc_inc (return value
      # discarded -- BoxShare is void beyond the refcount side effect),
      # then pushes the SAME ptr back unchanged, matching interp.patlang's
      # own BoxShare exactly. ptr is needed both as bm_rc_inc's own arg
      # and as this instruction's own result, so it's Stored once and
      # Loaded twice (an ordinary Call consumes/pops its arg off the
      # stack, so the first Load is consumed by the call and a second
      # Load is needed to leave the pointer behind afterward).
      let ptr_l = bm_nc_local(block_name, i, "p")
      let out = list_push(out, ["Store", ptr_l])
      let out = list_push(out, ["Load", ptr_l])
      let out = list_push(out, ["Call", "bm_rc_inc", 1])
      let out = list_push(out, ["Store", bm_nc_prefix(block_name, "__bm_discard")])
      let out = list_push(out, ["Load", ptr_l])
    elif op == 23684 then # "Emit"
      # Pops (globals, event_name, payload) -- same order as interp
      # .patlang's own bm_st_pop chain (globals on top, popped first;
      # see block_ir.patlang's own Phase 10 header). Unlike every other
      # opcode in this file, Emit does NOT inline anything here -- by the
      # time this dispatch runs, every handler registered anywhere in the
      # program has ALREADY been compiled as its own real, separate
      # FuncIR (see bm_nc_build_handler_funcirs below), reachable by an
      # ordinary native Call. This dispatch only needs to emit the
      # RUNTIME dispatch: a chain of string comparisons against
      # bi_program_events(prog)'s own compile-time event table (an
      # event_name computed at runtime, e.g. a Var, works exactly like a
      # literal -- both are just a string value compared with rt_str_eq
      # at runtime, no compile-time-literal restriction needed at all),
      # each matching branch calling every one of that event's own
      # registered handler FuncIRs in registration order, threading each
      # one's own returned "__globals" into the next -- exactly
      # interp.patlang's own cur_globals loop, translated. An event_name
      # matching NOTHING falls through with "__globals" already correct
      # (never touched), matching bi_run_block's own "handler_names ==
      # false -> [] -> zero iterations -> cur_globals stays globals_val"
      # exactly.
      let globals_l = bm_nc_local(block_name, i, "g")
      let event_l = bm_nc_local(block_name, i, "e")
      let payload_l = bm_nc_local(block_name, i, "p")
      let cur_l = bm_nc_local(block_name, i, "cg")
      let out = list_push(out, ["Store", globals_l])
      let out = list_push(out, ["Store", event_l])
      let out = list_push(out, ["Store", payload_l])
      let events = bi_program_events(prog)
      let en = list_len(events)
      let done_positions = []
      let ei = 0
      while ei < en do
        let entry = list_get(events, ei)
        let ev_name = list_get(entry, 0)
        let handler_names = list_get(entry, 1)
        let out = list_push(out, ["Load", event_l])
        let out = list_push(out, ["Const", "str", ev_name])
        let out = list_push(out, ["Call", "rt_str_eq", 2])
        let out = list_push(out, ["__RawJumpIfFalse", 0])
        let jif_next_pos = list_len(out) - 1
        let out = list_push(out, ["Load", globals_l])
        let out = list_push(out, ["Store", cur_l])
        let hn = list_len(handler_names)
        let hj = 0
        while hj < hn do
          let hname = list_get(handler_names, hj)
          # Push args in handler_params' own written order (event_name,
          # event_data, __globals) -- matches lower_expr's own "push in
          # written order, argc of them" Call convention exactly, same as
          # every other real Call in this file.
          let out = list_push(out, ["Const", "str", ev_name])
          let out = list_push(out, ["Load", payload_l])
          let out = list_push(out, ["Load", cur_l])
          let out = list_push(out, ["Call", hname, 3])
          let out = list_push(out, ["Store", cur_l])
          let hj = hj + 1
        end
        let out = list_push(out, ["Load", cur_l])
        let out = list_push(out, ["Store", bm_nc_prefix(block_name, "__globals")])
        let out = list_push(out, ["__RawJump", 0])
        let done_positions = list_push(done_positions, list_len(out) - 1)
        let next_target = list_len(out)
        let out = list_set(out, jif_next_pos, ["__RawJumpIfFalse", next_target])
        let ei = ei + 1
      end
      let after_target = list_len(out)
      let dpi = 0
      while dpi < list_len(done_positions) do
        let out = list_set(out, list_get(done_positions, dpi), ["__RawJump", after_target])
        let dpi = dpi + 1
      end
    elif op == 550066 then # "BuildList"
      # Direct passthrough -- codegen_x64.patlang's own real "BuildList"
      # instruction already exists and already works (confirmed by this
      # file's own init_instrs/HandlerNew/assoc-set use of it), so
      # block-model's own new BuildList opcode (Phase 18) needs no
      # translation logic at all beyond forwarding its own operand.
      let out = list_push(out, ["BuildList", list_get(instr, 1)])
    elif op == 700058 then # "CallHost"
      # Direct passthrough, same reasoning -- but genuinely NOT
      # guaranteed to link/assemble for every possible name the way
      # BuildList's own passthrough is: codegen_x64.patlang enforces its
      # own "OS-boundary allowlist" for CallHost targets (confirmed
      # directly, this file's own Print/ContractFail comments above), so
      # a generic host-function name from Phase 18's own interpreter-side
      # fallback (interp_call_host covers far more names than that
      # allowlist does) may be REJECTED at native build time -- a real,
      # already-existing, already-loud safety net (a clear compile-time
      # rejection, not silent corruption), left to fire on its own rather
      # than pre-filtered here. Not attempted or claimed complete for
      # every possible CallHost name; genuinely untested beyond whatever
      # names this phase's own native_codegen_check.sh scenarios cover.
      let out = list_push(out, ["CallHost", list_get(instr, 1), list_get(instr, 2)])
    elif op == 605222 then # "ThreadSpawn"
      # Issue #184: the closure value (a block-model List ["__closure",
      # name, captured], #182) is already on the stack. Converts it to a
      # REAL native closure (MakeClosure, codegen_x64.patlang's own
      # already-existing instruction -- no new asm emission needed here
      # at all) pointed at bm_closure_entry (lower.patlang's own
      # bm_inject_closure_entry_helper), then calls the real x64 runtime's
      # thread_spawn directly, unprefixed -- it is not a block-model-
      # declared function, it is auto-bundled from x64_runtime.patlang
      # (via rt_names/extern_names, same as every other runtime function)
      # and reached the same way the real engine's own thread_spawn
      # (closure) call already is. thread_spawn returns a plain int id,
      # not the [value, globals] pair a call to a block-model callee
      # would, so no bm_nc_emit_call_unpack here.
      #
      # THREE captured values, not two -- a real, reproduced bug found
      # verifying this (segfault after the spawned thread's own dispatch
      # ran): EVERY block-model function, bm_closure_entry included,
      # always gets an implicit trailing __globals parameter appended
      # (bi_block_params, Fork B's own universal convention) -- its real
      # compiled FuncIR takes (name, captured, __globals), not just the
      # two params its own source declares. The native thread trampoline
      # pushes exactly as many args as MakeClosure's own captured_count
      # says; omitting the third left bm_closure_entry reading garbage
      # off the stack for its own __globals. Supplies a fresh, empty
      # globals list -- matching parallel_map's own "__globals starts
      # empty, a worker's changes are not written back" convention
      # (parallel_map.feature), the same reasoning applying here since
      # thread_spawn is likewise genuine concurrency, not fiber-style
      # cooperative yielding.
      let tc = bm_nc_local(block_name, i, "tc")
      let tc_name = bm_nc_local(block_name, i, "tc_name")
      let tc_captured = bm_nc_local(block_name, i, "tc_captured")
      let tc_globals = bm_nc_local(block_name, i, "tc_globals")
      let out = list_push(out, ["Store", tc])
      let out = list_push(out, ["Load", tc])
      let out = list_push(out, ["Const", "num", "1"])
      let out = list_push(out, ["CallHost", "list_get", 2])
      let out = list_push(out, ["Store", tc_name])
      let out = list_push(out, ["Load", tc])
      let out = list_push(out, ["Const", "num", "2"])
      let out = list_push(out, ["CallHost", "list_get", 2])
      let out = list_push(out, ["Store", tc_captured])
      let out = list_push(out, ["BuildList", "0"])
      let out = list_push(out, ["Store", tc_globals])
      let out = list_push(out, ["Load", tc])
      let out = list_push(out, ["Const", "num", "3"])
      let out = list_push(out, ["CallHost", "list_get", 2])
      let out = list_push(out, ["Call", "rt_check_spawn_arity", 1])
      let out = list_push(out, ["Store", bm_nc_prefix(block_name, "__bm_discard")])
      let out = list_push(out, ["Load", tc_name])
      let out = list_push(out, ["Load", tc_captured])
      let out = list_push(out, ["Load", tc_globals])
      let out = list_push(out, ["MakeClosure", bm_nc_fn_name("bm_closure_entry"), [tc_name, tc_captured, tc_globals]])
      let out = list_push(out, ["Call", "thread_spawn", 1])
    elif op == 938836 then # "Call"
      # Issue #173: a declared function called with a return value. The
      # callee is its own separate FuncIR (bm_nc_build_callee_funcirs),
      # reached by an ordinary native Call exactly as Emit reaches a
      # handler; the args and the current __globals are already on the
      # stack in the callee's own declared-param order (lower.patlang's
      # own "args, then Load __globals" convention). It returns the
      # two-element list [value, final_globals]; unpack both here.
      #
      # GitHub #215: UNLESS the callee is provably pure (bm_nc_compute_
      # pure_fns, stashed by bm_to_real_funcir), in which case it was
      # compiled with no __globals parameter and no wrap on return (see
      # "callee_pure" mode below) -- its bare value is already exactly
      # where this call site needs it, on top of the stack. The single
      # instruction just before this one in `out` is always that Load's
      # own translation (bm_lower_declared_call emits "push args, Load
      # __globals, Call" as one fixed, uninterrupted sequence -- lower.
      # patlang's own header, Phase 4 -- so nothing else can have been
      # appended to `out` in between): dropping exactly that one entry
      # and reducing argc by 1 undoes pushing a value the pure callee was
      # never going to read anyway.
      let callee_name = list_get(instr, 1)
      let argc = list_get(instr, 2)
      if bm_list_contains(get("__vars", "bm_pure_fns"), callee_name) then
        let trimmed = []
        let oi = 0
        while oi < (list_len(out) - 1) do
          let trimmed = list_push(trimmed, list_get(out, oi))
          let oi = oi + 1
        end
        let out = list_push(trimmed, ["Call", bm_nc_fn_name(callee_name), argc - 1])
      else
        let out = list_push(out, ["Call", bm_nc_fn_name(callee_name), argc])
        let out = bm_nc_emit_call_unpack(out, block_name, i)
      end
    elif op == 131889 then # "ReturnValue"
      # Issue #173: pops one value and ends the CURRENT function with it.
      # Inside a callee FuncIR that means returning [value, globals] (the
      # convention Call above unpacks); in `main` or a handler there is no
      # caller to return a value to, so the value is discarded and the
      # function ends the way it would have by running off its own end.
      if mode == "callee" then
        let out = list_push(out, ["Load", bm_nc_prefix(block_name, "__globals")])
        let out = list_push(out, ["BuildList", 2])
        let out = list_push(out, ["Return"])
      elif mode == "callee_pure" then
        # GitHub #215: a provably pure function returns its value bare --
        # no __globals to load (it was never given one as a param, see
        # bm_nc_build_callee_funcirs), no BuildList wrap, matching the
        # call site's own skip of bm_nc_emit_call_unpack above.
        let out = list_push(out, ["Return"])
      elif mode == "handler" then
        let out = list_push(out, ["Store", bm_nc_prefix(block_name, "__bm_discard")])
        let out = list_push(out, ["Load", bm_nc_prefix(block_name, "__globals")])
        let out = list_push(out, ["Return"])
      else
        let out = list_push(out, ["Store", bm_nc_prefix(block_name, "__bm_discard")])
        let out = list_push(out, ["Const", "unit", ""])
        let out = list_push(out, ["Return"])
      end
    elif op == 915129 then # "CallDynamic"
      let out = bm_nc_emit_call_dynamic(out, block_name, i, list_get(instr, 1), prog)
    else
      bm_nc_unsupported("instruction hash '" + op + "'")
    end
    let i = i + 1
  end
  let index_map = list_push(index_map, list_len(out))
  # Issue #173 (extended, issue #180): a block that runs off its own end is a
  # HALT in the interpreter (bi_run_from returns right there), never a
  # fall-through into whichever block happens to come next in the flattened
  # array -- pending's own append order (bm_lower_while pushes an outer
  # loop's exit block AFTER any exit blocks a nested/sequential construct
  # built while lowering it) does not match physical execution order, so
  # relying on adjacency is wrong in general, not just for "callee". A real,
  # confirmed bug this exposed: two sequential top-level while loops -- the
  # first loop's own exit block is just "enter the second loop", which
  # bm_lower_while pushes to `pending` BEFORE the second loop's own real
  # exit block is pushed, so the second loop's exit (which should end the
  # program) physically preceded the first loop's trampoline and fell
  # through into it, re-entering the second loop forever. This was already
  # fixed for "callee" (Phase 21); the same fallthrough risk exists for
  # "main" and "handler", which previously relied on an unconditional
  # terminator appended ONCE after the whole flattened array -- correct only
  # when the true last block in program order happens to be the one that
  # naturally ends bare, not in general. Appended AFTER the end-of-block
  # sentinel above, so a Jump/JumpIfFalse whose target is "one past the last
  # instruction" (the sentinel) lands on this same halt, exactly as the
  # interpreter's own "pc == length" ends the block.
  if mode == "callee" then
    let out = list_push(out, ["Const", "unit", ""])
    let out = list_push(out, ["Load", bm_nc_prefix(block_name, "__globals")])
    let out = list_push(out, ["BuildList", 2])
    let out = list_push(out, ["Return"])
  elif mode == "callee_pure" then
    # GitHub #215: same reasoning as ReturnValue's own "callee_pure"
    # branch above -- a pure function's implicit end-of-block halt
    # returns a bare unit, no wrap.
    let out = list_push(out, ["Const", "unit", ""])
    let out = list_push(out, ["Return"])
  elif mode == "main" then
    let out = list_push(out, ["Const", "unit", ""])
    let out = list_push(out, ["Return"])
  elif mode == "handler" then
    let out = list_push(out, ["Load", bm_nc_prefix(block_name, "__globals")])
    let out = list_push(out, ["Return"])
  end
  return [out, index_map]
end

# Bin/Un operator hashes (also bm_op_hash'd by lower.patlang's rewrite)
# need to become real operator STRINGS again for the real FuncIR's own
# Bin/Un instructions, which self_hosting/lib/codegen_x64.patlang parses
# as literal "+"/"-"/etc.
make a function called bm_nc_bin_op_string takes h returns s
  if h == 177616 then
    return "+"
  elif h == 177618 then
    return "-"
  elif h == 177615 then
    return "*"
  elif h == 177620 then
    return "/"
  elif h == 177610 then
    return "%"
  elif h == 861968 then
    return "=="
  elif h == 861044 then
    return "!="
  elif h == 177633 then
    return "<"
  elif h == 861935 then
    return "<="
  elif h == 177635 then
    return ">"
  elif h == 862001 then
    return ">="
  elif h == 52935 then
    return "band"
  elif h == 486917 then
    return "bor"
  elif h == 78029 then
    return "bxor"
  elif h == 505193 then
    return "shl"
  elif h == 505199 then
    return "shr"
  end
  bm_nc_unsupported("Bin operator hash '" + h + "'")
  return "+"
end

make a function called bm_nc_un_op_string takes h returns s
  if h == 499987 then
    return "not"
  elif h == 177618 then
    return "-"
  end
  bm_nc_unsupported("Un operator hash '" + h + "'")
  return "not"
end

make a function called bm_nc_assoc_get takes assoc, key returns v
  let n = list_len(assoc)
  let i = 0
  while i < n do
    let entry = list_get(assoc, i)
    if list_get(entry, 0) == key then
      return list_get(entry, 1)
    end
    let i = i + 1
  end
  return false
end

# Phase 19 (Emit): the transitive closure of block names reachable from
# `root_name` by following still-hashed "JumpBlock" (974555) edges --
# root_name itself plus every synthesized descendant (a loop head/exit,
# etc.) a handler's own body can reach. This closure is EXACTLY "this
# handler's own family": a synthesized descendant's name is always a
# fresh, globally-unique bm_next_synth_name() value referenced from
# nowhere except the ONE body that created it (confirmed directly
# against lower.patlang's own construction: bm_lower_while is only ever
# called with a SPECIFIC handler/function's own statement list in
# scope), so this can never accidentally pull in a block belonging to
# `main` or to a DIFFERENT handler. An index-based worklist over
# `visited` itself (rather than a separate queue) avoids needing a "pop
# front" list primitive.
make a function called bm_nc_collect_block_closure takes prog, root_name returns names
  let visited = [root_name]
  let i = 0
  while i < list_len(visited) do
    let cur_name = list_get(visited, i)
    let blk = bi_find_block(prog, cur_name)
    let instrs = bi_block_instrs(blk)
    let n = list_len(instrs)
    let j = 0
    while j < n do
      let instr = list_get(instrs, j)
      if list_get(instr, 0) == 974555 then # "JumpBlock"
        let target = list_get(instr, 1)
        if not bm_list_contains(visited, target) then
          let visited = list_push(visited, target)
        end
      end
      let j = j + 1
    end
    let i = i + 1
  end
  return visited
end

# Every handler root registered anywhere in the event table, each paired
# with its own closure (bm_nc_collect_block_closure above) -- computed
# ONCE up front so both family-partitioning (bm_to_real_funcir) and
# handler-FuncIR construction (bm_nc_build_handler_funcirs) can reuse the
# same closures without recomputing them.
make a function called bm_nc_handler_closures takes prog returns pairs
  let events = bi_program_events(prog)
  let en = list_len(events)
  let pairs = []
  let seen = []
  let ei = 0
  while ei < en do
    let handler_names = list_get(list_get(events, ei), 1)
    let hn = list_len(handler_names)
    let hi = 0
    while hi < hn do
      let root = list_get(handler_names, hi)
      if not bm_list_contains(seen, root) then
        let seen = list_push(seen, root)
        let pairs = list_push(pairs, [root, bm_nc_collect_block_closure(prog, root)])
      end
      let hi = hi + 1
    end
    let ei = ei + 1
  end
  return pairs
end

# Which handler (if any) owns `name`, per the closures above -- false if
# `name` belongs to `main` instead (not registered under any handler's
# own closure).
make a function called bm_nc_owning_handler takes closures, name returns owner
  let n = list_len(closures)
  let i = 0
  while i < n do
    let pair = list_get(closures, i)
    if bm_list_contains(list_get(pair, 1), name) then
      return list_get(pair, 0)
    end
    let i = i + 1
  end
  return false
end

# The core flatten pass (Phase 9's own original bm_to_real_funcir body,
# generalized): concatenate `blocks`' own translated instrs (in the
# order given -- callers pass them in bi_program_blocks(prog)'s own
# original relative order, which already keeps each function/handler's
# own synthesized descendants correctly contiguous and correctly
# ORDERED, exactly the order Phase 9's own single-family version always
# relied on for "falls off the end lands in the right place" to hold),
# record each block's absolute start pc (offset by init_instrs' own
# length), then a final pass rewrites block-LOCAL Jump/JumpIfFalse
# targets to absolute indices and resolves __JumpBlockTarget/__RawJump*
# placeholders -- unchanged from Phase 9/13/19's own original logic,
# just no longer hardcoded to bi_program_blocks(prog) as a whole.
make a function called bm_nc_flatten_blocks takes prog, blocks, init_instrs, print_op, mode returns flat
  let bn = list_len(blocks)
  let translated = []
  let starts = []
  let running_len = list_len(init_instrs)
  let bi = 0
  while bi < bn do
    let blk = list_get(blocks, bi)
    let name = bi_block_name(blk)
    let result = bm_nc_translate_block_instrs(name, bi_block_instrs(blk), print_op, prog, mode)
    let t = list_get(result, 0)
    let index_map = list_get(result, 1)
    let translated = list_push(translated, [name, t, index_map])
    let starts = list_push(starts, [name, running_len])
    let running_len = running_len + list_len(t)
    let bi = bi + 1
  end

  let flat = init_instrs
  let bi = 0
  while bi < bn do
    let entry = list_get(translated, bi)
    let name = list_get(entry, 0)
    let t = list_get(entry, 1)
    let index_map = list_get(entry, 2)
    let base = bm_nc_assoc_get(starts, name)
    let tn = list_len(t)
    let ti = 0
    while ti < tn do
      let instr = list_get(t, ti)
      let op = list_get(instr, 0)
      if (op == "Jump") or (op == "JumpIfFalse") then
        let mapped = list_get(index_map, list_get(instr, 1))
        let flat = list_push(flat, [op, base + mapped])
      elif op == "__JumpBlockTarget" then
        let target_base = bm_nc_assoc_get(starts, list_get(instr, 1))
        let flat = list_push(flat, ["Jump", target_base])
      elif (op == "__RawJump") or (op == "__RawJumpIfFalse") then
        # Phase 19 (Global*/Handler*/Emit loop/dispatch emission): a
        # target SYNTHESIZED entirely within bm_nc_translate_block_instrs
        # 's own emission of one instruction's own expansion, not a real
        # block-model Jump/JumpIfFalse at all -- its own operand is
        # ALREADY a valid index into THIS block's own translated stream
        # `t` (computed via list_len(out) as the sequence was built,
        # backpatched the same way bm_lower_stmt's own If/Match cases
        # already do), so it needs only `+base`, never an index_map
        # lookup (which would misinterpret it as an ORIGINAL,
        # pre-expansion block-model instruction index and remap it to
        # the wrong position).
        let real_op = "Jump"
        if op == "__RawJumpIfFalse" then
          let real_op = "JumpIfFalse"
        end
        let flat = list_push(flat, [real_op, base + list_get(instr, 1)])
      else
        let flat = list_push(flat, instr)
      end
      let ti = ti + 1
    end
    let bi = bi + 1
  end
  # Issue #174: the Rust-source backend (print_op == "CallHost", the
  # wasm32-wasip1 path) has no x64 runtime, so retarget the runtime calls the
  # translations above emit.
  if print_op == "CallHost" then
    return bm_nc_wasm_rewrite(flat)
  end
  return flat
end

# Issue #174: retargets a flattened instruction stream for the Rust-source /
# WASM backend. The translations above emit calls to x64_runtime.patlang's
# own rt_* functions (assoc-list walks for Global*/Handler*, event and
# apply() dispatch chains) and to runtime-defined fact/query/type_of/
# read_file/write_file; none exist under emit_program_rs, so each becomes
# its ordinary host equivalent. One instruction is replaced by exactly one,
# so no jump target moves. The heap (Box*) and fibers are native-only BY
# DESIGN -- they depend on heap_chunk.obj and the x64 runtime's own stacks --
# and fail HERE, at translation time, naming the reason, rather than at
# program start (#181) or partway through a run.
make a function called bm_nc_wasm_rewrite takes flat returns out
  let n = list_len(flat)
  let out = []
  let i = 0
  while i < n do
    let instr = list_get(flat, i)
    if list_get(instr, 0) == "Call" then
      let nm = list_get(instr, 1)
      if nm == "rt_list_len" then
        let out = list_push(out, ["CallHost", "list_len", 1])
      elif nm == "rt_list_get" then
        let out = list_push(out, ["CallHost", "list_get", 2])
      elif nm == "rt_list_push" then
        let out = list_push(out, ["CallHost", "list_push", 2])
      elif nm == "rt_str_eq" then
        let out = list_push(out, ["Bin", "=="])
      elif (nm == "fact") or (nm == "query") or (nm == "type_of") or (nm == "read_file") or (nm == "write_file") then
        let out = list_push(out, ["CallHost", nm, list_get(instr, 2)])
      elif nm == "fiber_yield" then
        bm_nc_unsupported("FiberYield under WASM -- fibers need the x64 runtime's own stack switching")
        let out = list_push(out, instr)
      elif (substr(nm, 0, 3) == "bm_") and (substr(nm, 0, 6) != "bm_fn_") then
        bm_nc_unsupported("'" + nm + "' under WASM -- Box instructions need the native refcounted heap (heap_chunk.obj), which the Rust-source backend does not have")
        let out = list_push(out, instr)
      else
        let out = list_push(out, instr)
      end
    elif (list_get(instr, 0) == "CallHost") and ((list_get(instr, 1) == "bm_budgeted_run") or (list_get(instr, 1) == "budget_check")) then
      bm_nc_unsupported("'" + list_get(instr, 1) + "' (budgeted blocks, #176) under WASM -- they run in a fiber, and the Rust-source backend has no fiber host functions")
      let out = list_push(out, instr)
    elif (list_get(instr, 0) == "CallHost") and (list_get(instr, 1) == "parallel_map") then
      bm_nc_unsupported("'parallel_map' (#176) under WASM -- its worker is looked up by name among the Rust-source program's own functions, which the block-model FuncIRs are not")
      let out = list_push(out, instr)
    else
      let out = list_push(out, instr)
    end
    let i = i + 1
  end
  return out
end

# The blocks of a function's JumpBlock family in the order the flattener needs
# them: the ROOT block first, because a flattened function starts executing at
# pc 0. Program order is not enough -- a function that tail-calls another
# declared EARLIER in the source used to start inside the callee (issue #180).
make a function called bm_nc_ordered_members takes all_blocks, member_names, root returns ordered
  let ordered = []
  let n = list_len(all_blocks)
  let i = 0
  while i < n do
    let blk = list_get(all_blocks, i)
    if bi_block_name(blk) == root then
      let ordered = list_push(ordered, blk)
    end
    let i = i + 1
  end
  let i = 0
  while i < n do
    let blk = list_get(all_blocks, i)
    if (bi_block_name(blk) != root) and bm_list_contains(member_names, bi_block_name(blk)) then
      let ordered = list_push(ordered, blk)
    end
    let i = i + 1
  end
  return ordered
end

# Phase 19 (Emit): one real, separate FuncIR per handler root -- what
# lets Emit translate to an ordinary native Call/Return instead of
# needing any new call-with-return mechanism in this backend. Each
# handler's own params are its own bm_nc_prefix'd "event_name"/
# "event_data"/"__globals" (matching what bm_nc_translate_block_instrs
# already prefixes Load/Store references to inside the handler's own
# body -- the incoming Call args must land in the SAME prefixed names
# the body expects to Load them from), and it ends by Loading and
# returning its own final "__globals" -- exactly the value
# interp.patlang's own Emit case reads back out of bi_run_block's
# "halt" outcome (bm_loc_get(final_handler_locs, "__globals")) once the
# handler naturally finishes.
make a function called bm_nc_build_handler_funcirs takes prog, closures, print_op returns funcirs
  let all_blocks = bi_program_blocks(prog)
  let abn = list_len(all_blocks)
  let cn = list_len(closures)
  let funcirs = []
  let ci = 0
  while ci < cn do
    let pair = list_get(closures, ci)
    let root = list_get(pair, 0)
    let member_names = list_get(pair, 1)
    let hblocks = bm_nc_ordered_members(all_blocks, member_names, root)
    let h_flat = bm_nc_flatten_blocks(prog, hblocks, [], print_op, "handler")
    let h_flat = list_push(h_flat, ["Load", bm_nc_prefix(root, "__globals")])
    let h_flat = list_push(h_flat, ["Return"])
    # docs/plans/general-refcounting.md, Phase 2: the same pass ordinary
    # callee functions and main already get, extended to handlers too.
    if bm_nc_closure_is_single_block(hblocks) then
      let h_flat = bm_nc_insert_string_rc_decs(root, h_flat)
    end
    let params = [bm_nc_prefix(root, "event_name"), bm_nc_prefix(root, "event_data"), bm_nc_prefix(root, "__globals")]
    let funcirs = list_push(funcirs, ["FuncIR", root, params, h_flat, []])
    let ci = ci + 1
  end
  return funcirs
end

# Issue #173: one real, separate FuncIR per Call target (bm_nc_call_roots),
# named bm_nc_fn_name(root), whose params are the ROOT block's own declared
# params (bi_block_params -- "__globals" already last) under the same
# bm_nc_prefix its own body Loads them from, so the incoming native Call
# args land in the names the body expects. Its body is the root's whole
# JumpBlock-reachable family flattened in "callee" mode, where every block
# ends with an explicit halt returning [unit, own __globals] and a
# ReturnValue returns [value, own __globals] -- the [value, globals] pair a
# call site unpacks. Blocks keep bi_program_blocks(prog)'s own relative
# order, the same "descendants stay contiguous and correctly ordered"
# property the handler builder above relies on.
# docs/plans/general-refcounting.md, Phase 2 (narrowest slice, per the
# plan's own staging decision -- "only refcount a string that's a
# direct result of one of the 8 producer calls, stored in exactly one
# never-reassigned/never-passed local, decremented at function exit").
# True now because rt_string_new routes string allocation through
# bm_alloc: this is the FIRST increment that actually reclaims memory --
# everything in this plan before this point only avoided UNNECESSARY
# allocation cost; nothing yet decremented a string's own refcount, so
# every string ever built still sat at refcount 1 forever (the same
# bump-forever growth as before this plan started, just with an unused
# header).
#
# Deliberately narrow: only a function whose ENTIRE closure is a single,
# branch-free block (no internal Jump/JumpIfFalse at all, not merely "no
# JumpBlock") is considered. That guarantees exactly ONE reachable
# terminal Return/ReturnValue: bm_nc_flatten_blocks's own tail-fallback
# Return, appended unconditionally after every root, is provably
# unreachable dead code in this specific shape (nothing can jump past
# the real terminal when there is no Jump/JumpIfFalse anywhere in the
# block), so "insert the decrement right before that one terminal" is
# unambiguous. A branching function (an if/else with its own early
# return, for example) would need the decrement duplicated at EACH exit
# point -- deliberately deferred to a later, separate increment rather
# than attempted here.
make a function called bm_nc_string_producer_names returns names
  return ["rt_str_concat", "sb_str", "chr", "substr", "sc_char", "rt_unit_to_str", "rt_int_to_str", "rt_limb9_to_str", "rt_dquote_str"]
end

make a function called bm_nc_is_string_producer_call takes instr returns yes
  let op = list_get(instr, 0)
  if (op != "Call") and (op != "CallHost") then
    return false
  end
  return bm_list_contains(bm_nc_string_producer_names(), list_get(instr, 1))
end

# Single-block closures only -- a later increment, not this one, would
# need to handle a root whose closure spans more than one block (loops,
# via JumpBlock).
make a function called bm_nc_closure_is_single_block takes cblocks returns yes
  return list_len(cblocks) == 1
end

# Every name appearing in any MakeClosure's own captured-names list,
# anywhere in `instrs` -- a local this narrow pass must never decrement,
# since a closure might keep it alive past this function's own return.
make a function called bm_nc_closure_captured_names takes instrs returns names
  let names = []
  let n = list_len(instrs)
  let i = 0
  while i < n do
    let instr = list_get(instrs, i)
    if list_get(instr, 0) == "MakeClosure" then
      let captured = list_get(instr, 2)
      let ci = 0
      while ci < list_len(captured) do
        let names = list_push(names, list_get(captured, ci))
        let ci = ci + 1
      end
    end
    let i = i + 1
  end
  return names
end

# Every Return/ReturnValue index in `instrs`, ascending -- a genuine
# early return is allowed now (generalized from an earlier version of
# this pass that required exactly one, real or dead-tail): a candidate
# local only needs to dominate every one of them (bm_nc_local_
# dominates_all_terminals, just below), not for there to be only one at
# all. Includes bm_nc_flatten_blocks's own dead-code tail fallback,
# wherever it's genuinely unreachable -- inserting a decrement before
# dead code is harmless (never executed), so there's no need to
# distinguish it from a real terminal here.
make a function called bm_nc_find_all_terminal_indices takes instrs returns indices
  let indices = []
  let n = list_len(instrs)
  let i = 0
  while i < n do
    let op = list_get(list_get(instrs, i), 0)
    if (op == "Return") or (op == "ReturnValue") then
      let indices = list_push(indices, i)
    end
    let i = i + 1
  end
  return indices
end

# True when a local created at instruction index `p` (by a direct
# producer call immediately before a [Store, name] there) is
# GUARANTEED to have already been created by the time control reaches
# EVERY index in `terminals`. Given this pass only ever runs on a
# single-block closure (bm_nc_closure_is_single_block -- no loops; those
# always cross blocks via JumpBlock in this project's own architecture),
# the only way to reach a later instruction without running an earlier
# one is a forward Jump/JumpIfFalse whose own target skips past it. Two
# conditions, both required:
#   1. `p` is strictly before every terminal's own index -- if some
#      terminal sits AT OR BEFORE `p`, that path reaches it without `p`
#      ever having run at all. Confirmed directly (not assumed): a store
#      that happens only inside one branch, after an unrelated early
#      return in a DIFFERENT branch (`if cond then return 0 end; let s =
#      chr(n); return n`), has its own Store at index 9 while the early
#      return sits at index 5 -- 5 <= 9, correctly rejected entirely, no
#      decrement inserted anywhere for that local.
#   2. no Jump/JumpIfFalse instruction positioned BEFORE `p` targets an
#      index AFTER `p` -- otherwise some path could skip `p` entirely
#      and still reach a later terminal. A jump positioned AT OR AFTER
#      `p` can never skip `p` (p has already run to reach that jump at
#      all), so only earlier jumps matter.
# Confirmed directly in the other direction too: a local created
# UNCONDITIONALLY before any branching at all (`let s = chr(n); if cond
# then return 0 end; return n`, Store at index 2, terminals at 8/11/13,
# no jump positioned before index 2 at all) passes both conditions and
# is correctly treated as safe to decrement at every one of its own
# function's terminals, early return included.
make a function called bm_nc_local_dominates_all_terminals takes instrs, p, terminals returns yes
  let tn = list_len(terminals)
  let ti = 0
  while ti < tn do
    if list_get(terminals, ti) <= p then
      return false
    end
    let ti = ti + 1
  end
  let i = 0
  while i < p do
    let instr = list_get(instrs, i)
    let op = list_get(instr, 0)
    if (op == 212209) or (op == 34716) then # "Jump" or "JumpIfFalse"
      if list_get(instr, 1) > p then
        return false
      end
    end
    let i = i + 1
  end
  return true
end

# How many instructions get inserted at a given terminal t: one triple
# (Load/Call/Store) per eligible local, except whichever one (if any)
# is the value actually being returned right there.
make a function called bm_nc_rc_dec_count_at_terminal takes instrs, t, eligible_names returns count
  let returned_name = ""
  if t > 0 then
    let prev = list_get(instrs, t - 1)
    if list_get(prev, 0) == "Load" then
      let returned_name = list_get(prev, 1)
    end
  end
  let count = 0
  let ei = 0
  while ei < list_len(eligible_names) do
    if list_get(eligible_names, ei) != returned_name then
      let count = count + 1
    end
    let ei = ei + 1
  end
  return count
end

# How far original instruction index `idx` has shifted once every
# terminal's own insertion (see bm_nc_rc_dec_count_at_terminal) is
# accounted for -- the sum of insert_counts[k] for every terminal at or
# before idx. Needed to correct an EXISTING Jump/JumpIfFalse's own
# target (an ORIGINAL index) after this rewrite inserts instructions
# earlier in the stream: without this, a jump whose target lands at or
# after an earlier insertion point would land in the WRONG place post-
# rewrite, since everything from that point on has moved. Found and
# fixed this way, not assumed correct from the IR shape alone: a real
# native fixture (a genuine early return, alternating which branch runs)
# first measured nonzero heap growth where the whole point of this pass
# is exactly zero -- the interpreted IR dump used to design this pass
# only ever DISPLAYS the instruction list, it never executes a jump, so
# it could not have caught this; only a real measurement could, and did.
make a function called bm_nc_cumulative_shift_at takes terminals, insert_counts, idx returns shift
  let shift = 0
  let ti = 0
  while ti < list_len(terminals) do
    if list_get(terminals, ti) <= idx then
      let shift = shift + list_get(insert_counts, ti)
    end
    let ti = ti + 1
  end
  return shift
end

# The actual rewrite: finds every eligible local (direct producer
# provenance, exactly one Store, never closure-captured, dominating
# every terminal per bm_nc_local_dominates_all_terminals) and inserts a
# decrement for each, once per terminal, immediately before that
# terminal -- except at a specific terminal where the local is the
# value actually being returned THERE (a Load of that same name right
# before that specific Return/ReturnValue; a local can be returned at
# one terminal and still correctly decremented at a different one it
# also dominates). Every EXISTING Jump/JumpIfFalse target gets corrected
# via bm_nc_cumulative_shift_at, using the shift AT THE TARGET (not at
# the jump instruction's own position -- those answer different
# questions). Returns `instrs` completely unchanged if no eligible local
# is found at all -- purely additive, never a correctness risk to
# anything this pass doesn't recognize.
make a function called bm_nc_insert_string_rc_decs takes root, instrs returns out
  let terminals = bm_nc_find_all_terminal_indices(instrs)
  if list_len(terminals) == 0 then
    return instrs
  end
  let captured_names = bm_nc_closure_captured_names(instrs)
  let n = list_len(instrs)
  let eligible_names = []
  let i = 0
  while i < (n - 1) do
    let instr = list_get(instrs, i)
    if bm_nc_is_string_producer_call(instr) then
      let next = list_get(instrs, i + 1)
      if list_get(next, 0) == "Store" then
        let name = list_get(next, 1)
        if not bm_list_contains(captured_names, name) then
          let store_count = 0
          let sj = 0
          while sj < n do
            let sinstr = list_get(instrs, sj)
            if (list_get(sinstr, 0) == "Store") and (list_get(sinstr, 1) == name) then
              let store_count = store_count + 1
            end
            let sj = sj + 1
          end
          if store_count == 1 then
            if bm_nc_local_dominates_all_terminals(instrs, i + 1, terminals) then
              let eligible_names = list_push(eligible_names, name)
            end
          end
        end
      end
    end
    let i = i + 1
  end
  if list_len(eligible_names) == 0 then
    return instrs
  end
  let insert_counts = []
  let ti = 0
  while ti < list_len(terminals) do
    let t = list_get(terminals, ti)
    let insert_counts = list_push(insert_counts, bm_nc_rc_dec_count_at_terminal(instrs, t, eligible_names) * 3)
    let ti = ti + 1
  end
  let discard_name = bm_nc_prefix(root, "__bm_string_rc_discard")
  let out = []
  let i2 = 0
  while i2 < n do
    if bm_list_contains(terminals, i2) then
      let returned_name = ""
      if i2 > 0 then
        let prev = list_get(instrs, i2 - 1)
        if list_get(prev, 0) == "Load" then
          let returned_name = list_get(prev, 1)
        end
      end
      let ei = 0
      while ei < list_len(eligible_names) do
        let name = list_get(eligible_names, ei)
        if name != returned_name then
          let out = list_push(out, ["Load", name])
          let out = list_push(out, ["Call", "rt_string_rc_dec", 1])
          let out = list_push(out, ["Store", discard_name])
        end
        let ei = ei + 1
      end
    end
    let instr = list_get(instrs, i2)
    let op = list_get(instr, 0)
    if (op == "Jump") or (op == "JumpIfFalse") then
      let target = list_get(instr, 1)
      let new_target = target + bm_nc_cumulative_shift_at(terminals, insert_counts, target)
      let out = list_push(out, [op, new_target])
    else
      let out = list_push(out, instr)
    end
    let i2 = i2 + 1
  end
  return out
end

make a function called bm_nc_build_callee_funcirs takes prog, roots, print_op returns funcirs
  let all_blocks = bi_program_blocks(prog)
  let abn = list_len(all_blocks)
  let rn = list_len(roots)
  let pure_fns = get("__vars", "bm_pure_fns")
  let funcirs = []
  let ri = 0
  while ri < rn do
    let root = list_get(roots, ri)
    let member_names = bm_nc_collect_block_closure(prog, root)
    let cblocks = bm_nc_ordered_members(all_blocks, member_names, root)
    let root_params = bi_block_params(bi_find_block(prog, root))
    let is_pure = bm_list_contains(pure_fns, root)
    # GitHub #215: dropping "__globals" from EXTERNAL params only --
    # a real bug found and fixed while building this against the
    # existing suite (the loop-exit/tail-call/diamond-include scenarios
    # all regressed): a pure root can still contain INTERNAL blocks (a
    # while loop's own body/exit) that thread "__globals" between
    # THEMSELVES via ordinary JumpBlock params, same as every other
    # block-to-block value -- that is pure bookkeeping, nothing to do
    # with whether the FUNCTION itself ever touches an actual global,
    # and removing the incoming parameter broke it (the entry block had
    # nothing to Store into its own "__globals" local before its first
    # JumpBlock needed to forward it). Fixed the same way the overall
    # program's own entry block already handles this exact situation
    # (bm_to_real_funcir's own init_instrs, just above): seed a fresh
    # empty "__globals" at the start of the root's own body instead of
    # receiving one as a parameter, so every internal JumpBlock inside
    # this closure keeps working completely unchanged -- only the
    # EXTERNAL calling convention (no parameter, no wrapped return)
    # actually differs for a pure function.
    let flat_mode = "callee"
    let params_to_use = root_params
    let pure_init = []
    if is_pure then
      let flat_mode = "callee_pure"
      let trimmed = []
      let ti = 0
      while ti < (list_len(root_params) - 1) do
        let trimmed = list_push(trimmed, list_get(root_params, ti))
        let ti = ti + 1
      end
      let params_to_use = trimmed
      # Issue #216 (full fix, follow-up to #215, and to this fix's own
      # first, narrower attempt): the seed above existed ONLY to give an
      # internal JumpBlock something to forward when this root's own
      # closure has more than one block (a loop body/exit, for example --
      # see the comment above this one for why that seemed needed at
      # all). An EARLIER version of this fix made the seed conditional on
      # `list_len(cblocks) > 1`, closing the single-block case (a real,
      # confirmed `rt_heap_alloc(64)` per call via `[BuildList, 0]`,
      # x64_buildlist_asm/codegen_x64.patlang) but leaving multi-block
      # pure functions (bm_alloc/bm_freelist_pop/bm_rc_dec, each with
      # their own if) still paying it, since BOM's own universal "every
      # block forwards __globals to the next" convention (lower.patlang)
      # threads it between a pure root's OWN internal blocks
      # unconditionally, regardless of purity.
      #
      # The REAL fix is in bm_nc_translate_block_instrs's own JumpBlock
      # case, just above bm_nc_flatten_blocks in this file: for a pure
      # root, EVERY internal JumpBlock now drops "__globals" from its own
      # forwarded param list and pops the single Load that fed it
      # (lower.patlang always emits exactly one ["Load", "__globals"]
      # immediately before any JumpBlock/Call, so nothing else can have
      # been appended to `out` in between -- the same invariant the Call
      # case below already relies on for the EXTERNAL calling
      # convention). With that in place, NOTHING in a pure root's closure
      # ever Loads or Stores a "..._globals" local anymore, at any block,
      # for any root shape -- so the entry seed is now provably
      # unnecessary unconditionally, not just for single-block roots.
      # Confirmed, not assumed: self_hosting/block_model/refcounted_heap_
      # native_check.patlang's own "100000 cycles stay flat" scenario
      # (previously failing for exactly bm_alloc/bm_freelist_pop/
      # bm_rc_dec's own multi-block shape) now passes with zero growth.
    end
    let flat = bm_nc_flatten_blocks(prog, cblocks, pure_init, print_op, flat_mode)
    if bm_nc_closure_is_single_block(cblocks) then
      let flat = bm_nc_insert_string_rc_decs(root, flat)
    end
    let params = []
    let pi = 0
    while pi < list_len(params_to_use) do
      let params = list_push(params, bm_nc_prefix(root, list_get(params_to_use, pi)))
      let pi = pi + 1
    end
    let funcirs = list_push(funcirs, ["FuncIR", bm_nc_fn_name(root), params, flat, []])
    let ri = ri + 1
  end
  return funcirs
end

# Issue #181: true when any block anywhere contains a Box instruction (the
# only instructions that touch heap.patlang's own refcounted heap):
# BoxNew 258931, BoxGet 251305, BoxSet 264373, BoxSetUnchecked 902425,
# BoxShare 992319 -- the same opcode hashes interp.patlang dispatches on.
make a function called bm_nc_program_uses_heap takes prog returns yes
  let blocks = bi_program_blocks(prog)
  let bn = list_len(blocks)
  let bi = 0
  while bi < bn do
    let instrs = bi_block_instrs(list_get(blocks, bi))
    let n = list_len(instrs)
    let i = 0
    while i < n do
      let op = list_get(list_get(instrs, i), 0)
      if (op == 258931) or (op == 251305) or (op == 264373) or (op == 902425) or (op == 992319) then
        return true
      end
      let i = i + 1
    end
    let bi = bi + 1
  end
  return false
end

# The whole translation: partitions bi_program_blocks(prog) into `main`
# (every ordinary function/loop-descendant block, i.e. everything NOT
# owned by some handler's own closure) and one family per handler root,
# flattens `main` into a single "main" FuncIR exactly as Phase 9/13
# always did, and -- new in Phase 19 -- compiles every handler into its
# OWN separate FuncIR (bm_nc_build_handler_funcirs above), reachable via
# an ordinary native Call from Emit's own dispatch (see its own case in
# bm_nc_translate_block_instrs). Returns a LIST of FuncIRs (was a single
# FuncIR before Emit existed, since there was never more than one) --
# every caller must pass the WHOLE list into emit_program_x64's own
# `funcs` argument now, not just the first element.
make a function called bm_to_real_funcir takes prog, print_op returns funcirs
  # GitHub #215: computed ONCE, before main/handlers/every callee is
  # translated, so every Call site anywhere (not just inside callee
  # FuncIRs) can see the same answer. Stashed via set_var/get rather than
  # threaded as a new parameter through bm_nc_flatten_blocks's and bm_nc_
  # translate_block_instrs's whole call chain, matching this file's own
  # established convention for compile-time-only context that many
  # unrelated call sites need (bm_synth_counter, bm_current_fn, etc.) --
  # read-only for the rest of this function's own call tree, never
  # written again during it.
  let pure_roots = bm_nc_call_roots(prog)
  set_var("bm_pure_fns", bm_nc_compute_pure_fns(prog, pure_roots))
  let blocks = bi_program_blocks(prog)
  let bn = list_len(blocks)
  let closures = bm_nc_handler_closures(prog)

  # Issue #173: `main` holds exactly the blocks reachable from the ENTRY by
  # JumpBlock edges (its own family, the same closure notion a handler or a
  # Call target gets), in bi_program_blocks's own relative order. It used to
  # hold every block not owned by a handler, which was harmless while every
  # function was reached by JumpBlock (whose Stores bind the callee's
  # params) but is wrong for a function reached ONLY by a Call: its
  # parameters are bound at no site inside main, so codegen_x64's
  # undefined-variable check correctly rejected main's dead copy of it.
  # A Call target is compiled into its own FuncIR instead.
  let entry_family = bm_nc_collect_block_closure(prog, bi_program_entry(prog))
  let main_blocks = []
  let bi = 0
  while bi < bn do
    let blk = list_get(blocks, bi)
    if bm_list_contains(entry_family, bi_block_name(blk)) and (bm_nc_owning_handler(closures, bi_block_name(blk)) == false) then
      let main_blocks = list_push(main_blocks, blk)
    end
    let bi = bi + 1
  end

  # bi_run seeds the ENTRY block's own "__globals" specially
  # (bm_loc_set(bm_loc_new(), "__globals", []) in interp.patlang) --
  # nothing ever JumpBlocks INTO the entry block to populate it via an
  # ordinary Store, so without this init instruction, the entry block's
  # own prefixed "__globals" name would be read (via the forwarding
  # Load every block unconditionally has) before ever being written,
  # which codegen_x64.patlang's own undefined-variable check correctly
  # catches (confirmed directly: this exact error is what surfaced this
  # gap in the first place, not something anticipated in advance).
  let entry_name = bi_program_entry(prog)
  # Phase 19 (Box*): interp.patlang's own bm_ensure_heap_ready() lazily
  # calls bm_heap_init() exactly once, guarded by a get("__vars",
  # "bm_heap_ready") flag, before the FIRST BoxNew -- heap.patlang's own
  # free-list table (bm_freelist_table_addr's "__vars" entry) is
  # otherwise never set, and bm_alloc's own bm_freelist_pop ->
  # bm_freelist_slot_offset -> bm_freelist_table_addr chain reads it
  # unconditionally: skipping this init is a real, confirmed segfault
  # (peeking/poking through an uninitialized table address), not a
  # theoretical gap -- found by running this exact fixture before this
  # fix existed. The native path calls bm_heap_init() unconditionally
  # and eagerly at program start instead of lazily gating it on a flag:
  # simpler and equally correct (there is exactly one program start,
  # unlike bi_run's own single shared interpreter process that resets
  # "bm_heap_ready" to false per bi_run call), and cheap even for a
  # program that never touches a Box at all (one rt_heap_alloc(1024) for
  # the free-list table, nothing more).
  # Issue #181: the heap is initialized only when the program actually
  # contains a Box instruction. Doing it unconditionally (Phase 19) made
  # EVERY program depend on heap_chunk.obj's bm_heap_init, which the WASM
  # path (emit_program_rs, no heap chunk) does not have -- so every WASM
  # program failed at startup on a function it never used, silently, since
  # the WASM check is a standalone script nothing ran. A program without a
  # Box has no use for the heap, so the native path loses nothing.
  #
  # Ordering fix (found scoping docs/plans/general-refcounting.md's Phase
  # 1 checkpoint): bm_heap_init() used to run AFTER the [BuildList, 0]
  # seed just below, meaning that seed -- unconditionally the first thing
  # ANY program's own main does -- had always already claimed the heap's
  # first allocation by the time bm_heap_init ran. heap.patlang's own
  # bm_heap_fixed_ready() check (its "is the free-list table the FIRST
  # heap allocation, so every thread can find it with no lookup at all"
  # fast path) could therefore never be true for a real program with any
  # other startup code -- confirmed directly: even the single-line
  # program `print("x")` already shows a 64-byte heap bump before its own
  # first statement runs. Every bm_alloc/bm_rc_inc/bm_rc_dec call then
  # fell through to the slow, lock-plus-"__vars"-lookup path instead of
  # the lock-free one -- measured at roughly 700x slower per alloc+drop
  # cycle (5,000,000 cycles: 62ms expected-shape vs 43,656ms measured).
  # Calling bm_heap_init() FIRST, before the __globals seed, makes the
  # free-list table genuinely the first allocation whenever a program
  # uses Box at all, restoring the fast path for real.
  #
  # Unconditional again (docs/plans/general-refcounting.md, Phase 2):
  # issue #181's own reason for gating this on bm_nc_program_uses_heap
  # was that an earlier, truly unconditional version broke the WASM path
  # (emit_program_rs, no heap chunk linked) -- but bm_to_real_funcir is
  # ONLY ever reached from bm_patc_lower (--x64/--bm) or build_native.
  # patlang's own test tooling, NEVER from the WASM path (which lowers
  # via plain lower_program, not bm_lower_program, and never calls this
  # function at all), so that concern never actually applied here.
  # Needs to be unconditional again now that string allocation
  # (x64_runtime.patlang's own rt_string_new) also goes through bm_alloc
  # -- bm_nc_program_uses_heap only detects real Box IR instructions, so
  # any program that builds a string but never calls box_new (nearly
  # every real program) would otherwise never initialize the free-list
  # table at all. Confirmed directly: bm_alloc dereferencing that
  # uninitialized table is a real, reproduced segfault, not a
  # theoretical gap -- found building Phase 2's own first increment.
  let init_instrs = [["Call", "bm_heap_init", 0], ["Store", bm_nc_prefix(entry_name, "__bm_discard")]]
  let init_instrs = list_push(init_instrs, ["BuildList", 0])
  let init_instrs = list_push(init_instrs, ["Store", bm_nc_prefix(entry_name, "__globals")])

  let main_flat = bm_nc_flatten_blocks(prog, main_blocks, init_instrs, print_op, "main")
  let main_flat = list_push(main_flat, ["Const", "unit", ""])
  let main_flat = list_push(main_flat, ["Return"])
  # docs/plans/general-refcounting.md, Phase 2: the same pass ordinary
  # callee functions already get (bm_nc_build_callee_funcirs, below),
  # extended to main itself. Low-impact in practice -- a real program's
  # own main is usually multi-block (any top-level while loop makes it
  # so, via JumpBlock), and bm_nc_closure_is_single_block only accepts
  # exactly one -- but free, correct, and worth having for the genuinely
  # single-block case (a short script with no loop at all).
  if bm_nc_closure_is_single_block(main_blocks) then
    let main_flat = bm_nc_insert_string_rc_decs(entry_name, main_flat)
  end
  let main_funcir = ["FuncIR", "main", [], main_flat, []]

  let handler_funcirs = bm_nc_build_handler_funcirs(prog, closures, print_op)
  let funcirs = [main_funcir]
  let hi = 0
  while hi < list_len(handler_funcirs) do
    let funcirs = list_push(funcirs, list_get(handler_funcirs, hi))
    let hi = hi + 1
  end
  # Issue #173: one more separate FuncIR per Call target.
  let callee_funcirs = bm_nc_build_callee_funcirs(prog, pure_roots, print_op)
  let ci = 0
  while ci < list_len(callee_funcirs) do
    let funcirs = list_push(funcirs, list_get(callee_funcirs, ci))
    let ci = ci + 1
  end
  return funcirs
end

# Namespacing note: an earlier version of this file bundled
# x64_runtime.patlang's own source directly into the same compilation
# unit as the translated block-model program, and salted (prefixed)
# every one of its ~326 function names to avoid collisions with a
# block-model program's own names. Tried, and abandoned after a REAL
# link failure: codegen_x64.patlang hardcodes raw `call
# rt_bigint_from_i64`-style asm text directly for overflow-checked
# arithmetic (x64_binop_asm_dynamic and similar), invisible to any
# IR-level Call/MakeClosure rename -- confirmed via that file's own
# header comment on emit_program_x64's `extern_names` parameter, which
# exists SPECIFICALLY because "most runtime calls... are hand-assembled
# string literals... invisible to any IR-level call-graph walk."
# Salting would have needed to also chase down every one of those
# hardcoded references, an open-ended, easy-to-miss list.
#
# The build now used instead (self_hosting/block_model/bench/
# build_phase9_native.patlang) sidesteps this category of problem
# entirely: a real two-CHUNK build, linking against the already-built,
# already-cached self_hosting/build/x64_runtime.obj (never re-lowered
# here) via emit_program_x64's own `extern_names` mechanism -- true
# linker-level namespace separation, which can't silently miss a
# hardcoded reference the way textual renaming could, since an
# unresolved symbol is a loud link error, not quiet misbehavior.
# Mirrors self_hosting/build_x64_runtime.patlang's own established
# two-chunk convention exactly, not a new invention.
